{"id":"8d53d794-45e4-4138-9451-ca7755930baf","object":"chat.completion","created":1784816700,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = payload['required_symbols']\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n['parent']\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    candidates = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for node in candidates:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        current_children = [c for c in children.get(nid, []) if c in retained]\\n        if current_children:\\n            continue\\n        parent_id = node['parent']\\n        for child in current_children:\\n            children[parent_id].append(child)\\n        children[parent_id].remove(nid)\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'removal_order': removal_order}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the task. It's about \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" similar to Perses. We have a tree of nodes with parent, symbol, depth, removable flag, utility. We need to decide which nodes to remove, preserving required symbols and grammar validity? The problem mentions \"grammar_valid\" but the fixtures don't show grammar rules; it only shows nodes and required_symbols. Possibly the grammar is implicit: parent-child relationships define a tree, and we must ensure that after removal, the tree still \"covers\" the required symbols? The case has a set of nodes forming a tree. We need to return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order. That suggests we simulate a reduction algorithm: iteratively try to remove removable nodes (not required, not root, no retained children) in order of depth (deeper first), then by ID tie-breaker, and check if after removal, the required symbols are still present (i.e., for each required symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol that is not removed). Also maybe \"interestingness\" is preserved: but not defined? Possibly just that required symbols survive. And \"grammar_valid\": maybe the tree must remain a valid tree (connected) or that parent relationships are respected? If we remove a node, its children become children of its parent? In Perses, removal of a node attaches children to the node's parent. But here parent is given, and nodes have IDs. The input has parent field with IDs. We need to model removal: if we remove a node, its children become children of its parent. That's typical. So we need to maintain adjacency. The problem says: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" And \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we can only remove a node if it has no children that are retained (i.e., all its descendants in the currently retained tree are removed). That is like bottom-up reduction. We also have removable flag: only nodes with removable=True can be considered for removal.\n\nWe must return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order (list of node IDs removed in order). Also note the tie-break: deeper nodes first, then node ID as stable tie break.\n\nWe need to implement `solve(case)` that takes a case dict with payload containing nodes list and required_symbols. Return something like {\"retained_ids\": [...], \"removal_order\": [...]}. But the problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The exact output format? It says \"return a JSON-serializable object\". Probably a dict with those keys.\n\nWe must not mutate case. Use only standard library.\n\nLet's parse the fixture to understand structure. Each node has id, parent (null for root), symbol, depth, removable (bool), utility (float). Required_symbols is list of strings.\n\nAlgorithm: Build tree structure from nodes. Map id->node dict. For each node, store children list. Then we need to simulate removal process: we have a set of retained nodes initially all nodes. We'll process nodes in order of depth descending (deepest first), and for same depth, ascending node ID (since tie-break stable, but problem says \"use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth\", and \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we sort candidates by depth descending, then node ID ascending. For each node in that order, if it is removable (removable==True), not root (parent not None), not a required symbol (check if its symbol is in required_symbols? But careful: required_symbols is list of symbols that must be present. The node itself may have required symbol; we cannot remove it if its symbol is required? The problem says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So \"required symbol\" means a node whose symbol is in required_symbols? Or that symbol must remain represented, meaning we need at least one node with that symbol. But the wording \"a required symbol\" could mean a node that is itself a required symbol? Typically in Perses, you cannot remove a node that is the only representative of a required nonterminal. But here they say \"required_symbols\" list. I think they mean we cannot remove any node whose symbol is in required_symbols, regardless of duplicates. Because they say \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That implies nodes with symbol in required_symbols are never removable. But they also say \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So there is a check even if node is removable and not required symbol etc. So first, only nodes with removable=True, not root, symbol not in required_symbols, and no retained children (i.e., all its children are already removed) can be considered. Then before removal, we check that after removal, all required symbols are still represented (i.e., there is at least one retained node for each required symbol). Since we don't remove nodes with required symbol, that condition should be satisfied automatically, but maybe there could be only one node with that symbol and it's removable? But we already exclude nodes with required symbol. So maybe the condition is redundant? Unless required_symbols includes symbols that may have multiple nodes but we might remove all but one? The condition says \"all required symbols remain represented\" after tentative removal. So if a node has a symbol that is not required, but removing it might eliminate the last occurrence of some required symbol? That doesn't make sense because required symbols are not removed. So the check is likely to ensure that even if node's own symbol is not required, removal could affect representation of required symbols? Not directly. Maybe it's for the case where a node is required because it's the only parent of a required symbol? But the required symbols are just symbol names. The node itself might not be required, but its removal could orphan a required symbol? In Perses, if you remove a node, its children become children of its parent. So required symbols that are descendants are still present; they are just moved up. So removal doesn't remove required symbols. So I think the condition is simpler: we only remove nodes that are not required symbols themselves. But the problem statement says \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts: grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved, registered_candidate_order, stable_ties.\" So we need to ensure grammar validity: maybe the tree must remain a valid parse tree according to some grammar? But not given. Possibly the tree structure itself (parent-child) is the grammar; removal must not create invalid parent-child relationships? The mentioned \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" suggests we need to consider that after removal, the node's children are attached to parent, which is valid if the parent can have children of those symbols? Not specified. Since no grammar rules given, we assume any attachment is valid. So grammar_valid is trivially satisfied.\n\nInterestingness_preserved: The oracle is not provided. The only condition given is required_symbols. So likely interestingness is that all required symbols are present. So we check that.\n\nCandidate_order: deeper first, then ID tie-break.\n\nStable_ties: use node ID.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\n- Parse nodes list.\n- Build dict id_to_node, children dict: id -> list of child ids.\n- Initialize retained set with all node ids.\n- Build list of nodes that are removable (removable=True) and not root (parent is not None) and not required symbol (symbol not in required_symbols). Also they must have no retained children (but that changes as we remove; we need to process dynamically). Better to iterate repeatedly: we can sort all nodes by depth descending and id ascending. Then for each node in that order, check if it's removable, not root, symbol not in required_symbols, and all its children are already removed (i.e., not in retained set). If so, tentatively remove it: check if after removal, all required symbols are still represented (i.e., for each req_sym in required_symbols, there is at least one retained node with that symbol). Since we don't remove nodes with req sym, and children of removed node become children of parent (so they remain), the set of retained nodes only loses this node, and its symbol is not required. So representation of required symbols remains the same. So condition always true. But we can still do the check for safety.\n\nIf conditions satisfied, remove node: remove from retained set, and update children relationships? For future checks, we need to know that a node's children are gone. Since we only care about retained children, we can just check if any child id is in retained set. When we remove a node, its children remain but now their parent becomes the removed node's parent. So we need to update the children list of the parent to include the children of removed node? Actually, the problem doesn't specify that we need to track parent relationships after removal for future decisions. The condition \"node with retained children\" means a node that still has some retained child in the current tree. If we remove a node, its children become children of its parent, so those children are now retained children of the parent. So for future removal decisions, the parent might still have retained children (the grandchildren). So we need to maintain the tree dynamically. That means we should have a data structure that updates parent of children when a node is removed. Alternatively, we can simulate by checking ancestors: a node has retained children if any descendant (in original tree) that is still retained is directly or indirectly a child? But careful: after removal, the tree changes; a node's direct children may include those that were originally grandchildren. So we need to keep a mutable parent mapping. Simpler: maintain a dict of current parent for each node. Initially from input. When we remove a node, for each of its children (current children), set their parent to the node's current parent, and add them to the parent's children list. Also remove the node from children list of its parent. Then the node is removed from retained set. Then when checking \"no retained children\" for a candidate node, we just check if any of its current children are in retained set.\n\nWe also need to consider that removable flag is static: if removable==False, never remove.\n\nAlso note: the order of removal: we process nodes in sorted order (by depth desc, then id asc). But after removals, the depth of nodes may change (children become shallower). The problem says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" The original depth? Or current depth? Typically in Perses, you consider nodes in the original tree order (preorder) and depth is based on original tree. The fixture shows depth field, which is original depth in the tree. So we should use the original depth as given. That ensures deterministic order. So we sort by depth descending, then node id ascending. That order is fixed.\n\nNow we need to iterate through that list, and for each node, if it is still retained (we might have removed it earlier?), but we process each node only once. However, a node might become removable only after its children are removed. But since we process deeper nodes first (higher depth), children are deeper so they will be processed earlier. So when we encounter a node, its children (which are deeper) have already been considered for removal. That ensures that if a node has any retained children, they would be deeper nodes that were not removed? But if a child was not removable (e.g., required symbol), it remains, so the parent still has a retained child and cannot be removed. That's correct. So processing once in that order is sufficient, as long as we check that the node itself is still retained and conditions are met. However, after removing a node, its parent's retained children set changes (we add the removed node's children to parent). That could make the parent have retained children even if originally it had none? Actually, originally if parent had children that were removed, those children are gone. But the grandchildren become direct children of parent, so parent may still have retained children. That's fine; we don't need to reprocess parent because we already considered it earlier? Wait, parent has lower depth (shallower), so it appears later in the sorted list (since depth descending). So when we process parent later, its children (now including the grandchildren) are still considered retained children. So the condition \"no retained children\" will check current children. That is correct because we update parent's children list when we remove a node. So we need to update parent's children list.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\n- Build node_map: id -> node object (with attributes)\n- Build parent_map: id -> parent_id (from node['parent'])\n- Build children_map: id -> list of child ids (initially from nodes where parent is that id)\n- Build retained set of all ids.\n- Build sorted_candidates list of all node ids sorted by (-depth, id). Depth from node['depth'].\n- removal_order = []\n- For each nid in sorted_candidates:\n    node = node_map[nid]\n    if nid not in retained: continue (already removed)\n    if not node['removable']: continue\n    if node['parent'] is None: continue (root)\n    if node['symbol'] in required_symbols: continue\n    # Check no retained children\n    current_children = children_map.get(nid, [])\n    has_retained_children = any(cid in retained for cid in current_children)\n    if has_retained_children: continue\n    # Tentatively remove: check required symbols still represented\n    # But since we don't remove required symbol nodes, this is true.\n    # Still, we can compute representation.\n    # Remove node: update structures\n    # For each child in current_children:\n        child_parent = node_map[nid]['parent'] (which is current parent)\n        # Actually, when we remove node, children become children of node's parent.\n        new_parent_id = node['parent']\n        # Update parent of child\n        parent_map[child] = new_parent_id\n        # Add child to children_map of new_parent\n        children_map[new_parent_id].append(child)\n    # Remove node from children_map of its parent\n    old_parent_id = node['parent']\n    children_map[old_parent_id].remove(nid)\n    # Remove node from retained\n    retained.remove(nid)\n    removal_order.append(nid)\n- After loop, retained_ids = sorted(list(retained)) (by id? The problem says \"sorted retained IDs\" probably in ascending order)\n- Return dict with 'retained_ids' and 'removal_order'.\n\nNote: We must not mutate case. We'll extract data and modify our own structures.\n\nEdge cases: What if there are nodes with parent that is not in nodes? Not in fixture.\n\nCheck with the given examples? Not required to run, but we can reason.\n\nNow produce JSON output containing the implementation string.\n\nWe need to output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a string containing Python source code.\n\nWrite code accordingly.\n\nNote: The code must be self-contained, no imports? Can use standard library. Use json for serialization at the end? Actually solve returns a JSON-serializable object. We can return a dict.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\n    required_symbols = payload['required_symbols']\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        p = n['parent']\n        if p is not None:\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    candidates = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removal_order = []\n    for node in candidates:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if node['parent'] is None:\n            continue\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # check retained children\n        current_children = [c for c in children.get(nid, []) if c in retained]\n        if current_children:\n            continue\n        # Remove tentatively: check required symbols still present\n        # compute symbols in retained\n        # but we know no required symbol node is removed, so safe\n        parent_id = node['parent']\n        # Update children of parent: add current children\n        for child in current_children:\n            children[parent_id].append(child)\n            # also update parent reference? not needed for later checks? We need children map for parent's check later.\n            # Also we need to ensure that when we later check parent for removal, its children include these.\n        # Remove node from parent's children list\n        children[parent_id].remove(nid)\n        # Remove node from retained\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'removal_order': removal_order}\n\nBut note: When we update parent's children, we appended current children to children[parent_id]. However, those children may already be in children[parent_id]? No, because they were originally children of nid, not of parent_id. However, it's possible that some of those children already have parent_id as parent? No, because each node has only one parent. So it's fine. Also, we should remove nid from children[parent_id] (done). Also, we should update parent_map for the children? Not necessary for our algorithm since we only check children_map for retained children. But if we later check a node for removal, we depend on children_map to know its children. If we change parent of children, we should update parent_map accordingly. However, we don't use parent_map elsewhere except maybe to ensure we don't remove root. But we don't need parent_map after initial. So okay.\n\nBut there is a subtlety: after removing a node, its former children become children of parent. However, when we later consider the parent node for removal, we need to know its current children. We added the former children to children[parent_id], so that works. However, those children themselves have their own children (grandchildren) that are still in the tree. The children map for those children remains unchanged (their children list still contains their own children). That's fine.\n\nBut also, when we remove a node, we should also remove it from the children list of its parent (done). Good.\n\nNow about the order: The candidates list is sorted by original depth descending. That matches \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" and tie-break by id.\n\nOne more thing: The algorithm might remove nodes that are not removable due to retained children, but after removal of their children later, they could become removable. But since we process them earlier (deeper first), that's fine. For a node that becomes removable later because its children were removed, we would have already processed it earlier and skipped because it had retained children. But that means we miss that chance. However, since children are deeper, they are processed first. If a node's children are removed, that happens before we process the node (since node is shallower and appears later in the list). Wait: the list is sorted depth descending, so deeper nodes come first. So when we process a deep node, its parent (shallower) hasn't been processed yet. After we remove the deep node, the parent's children list is updated to include the removed node's children. But the parent itself is still in the candidate list (later). So when we later process the parent, its children map will reflect the current state. So that's correct. The parent's retained children are those that are still retained after removals. So if all its children (which may be grandchildren originally) are removed, it will have no retained children and can be removed.\n\nThus algorithm works.\n\nNeed to ensure we don't modify the input case. We create our own dicts.\n\nNow produce final JSON.\n\nLet's write the code as a string.\n\nImplementation code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\n    required_symbols = payload['required_symbols']\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        p = n['parent']\n        if p is not None:\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    candidates = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removal_order = []\n    for node in candidates:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if node['parent'] is None:\n            continue\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # check retained children\n        current_children = [c for c in children.get(nid, []) if c in retained]\n        if current_children:\n            continue\n        parent_id = node['parent']\n        # transfer children to parent\n        for child in current_children:\n            children[parent_id].append(child)\n        # remove node from parent's children list\n        children[parent_id].remove(nid)\n        # remove node from retained\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'removal_order': removal_order}\n```\n\nWe must embed this as a JSON string. Escape backslashes, newlines, quotes.\n\nNow produce final response exactly as required.\n\nNote: The response must be a single JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is the string of the Python source.\n\nLet's ensure no trailing spaces, etc. Use double quotes for JSON.\n\nWe'll generate the JSON string manually or using a script mentally. Since I'm AI, I can output the JSON directly.\n\nThe source code has newlines; we need to replace newline with \\n, backslash with \\\\, double quotes with \\\". Also, there are triple quotes? No.\n\nLet's produce the JSON string.\n\nWe'll write the code as:\n\n\"def solve(